{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789858854,"nonce":"d+9pO9dTJkEz3cNEAiN47ClLq28=","title":"Python attribute accesses check the same object for None once per access; the compiler kept every check","message":"A method reading self.x then self.y, or a loop body reading t.link after `while t is not None`, compares the object against a fresh zero constant before each read and raises AttributeError on the cold edge. richards' HandlerTask.fn had 9 such checks for 12 reads, schedule 6, qpkt 4. Nothing folded them: each compare used its own `const 0`, so CSE saw different keys, and no pass folded a branch on a condition a dominating branch had already decided.\n\nCSE now treats equal integer, boolean and null constants of one type as one operand, and a branch_fold pass after it folds a brcond whose condition a dominating branch settled (through `xor c, true` too). HandlerTask.fn keeps 3 checks, schedule 2, qpkt 2; richards on the Mac 0.87 -\u003e 0.78 ms per iteration.","files":null}]}